// spec.bot generated from spec.human. Do not edit by hand, change spec.human and regenerate.
// [path][line] = where the code lives, [path] is relative to the nearest folder that holds .git, or the parent of spec/ when no .git is found

#implementation [./package.json][1]
- implement a parser for onlyhuman skill syntax; this covers the *.human and *.bot spec language described here
- language is TypeScript, development requires Node >= 22.18, strict mode
- parser, linter and definition core are pure functions, no editor or fs dependency; @LSP and @CLI share them
- @Linter and @GoToDefinition work on *.bot as well as *.human
- do not call the onlyhuman cli while building this project, it is what is being created; read onlyhuman/SKILL.md for the rules, and check the work with npm test
- layout
  - src/types.ts // $Token $Node $Tokens types
  - src/tokenizer.ts // $Data -> $Tokens
  - src/parser.ts // $Tokens + $Structure -> parser -> ?Parsed
  - src/linter.ts // @Linter
  - src/definition.ts // @GoToDefinition
  - src/files.ts // fs access for sibling files, project root, pointers and imported specs, used by @CLI and @LSP
  - src/lsp/server.ts // @LSP
  - src/autocomplete.ts // @AutoComplete, a pure function used by @LSP
  - src/cli.ts // @CLI
  - src/skill.ts // @Skill copy operation
  - onlyhuman/SKILL.md // Codex skill instructions and tooling
  - dist/cli.js // bundled CLI published to npm
  - npm.sh // build, verify and publish the CLI
  - vscode/ // thin client, starts the lsp for *.human and *.bot
  - vscode/dist/ // bundled output of esbuild, what the .vsix ships
  - vscode/install-local.sh // bash script to build, package and install the extension locally
  - vscode/syntaxes/human.tmLanguage.json // grammar for *.human, see vscode.bot
  - vscode/syntaxes/robot.tmLanguage.json // same grammar for *.bot, language id onlyhuman-robot
  - tests/ // unit tests on the pure functions
  - README.md // project documentation and npm package page
  - LICENSE // MIT license for the repository
  - vscode/LICENSE // the same MIT license, shipped in the .vsix
- [path][line] numbers are targets and get updated after coding, applies to every header

# version control [./package.json][1]
- version 0.8.0 is applied to package.json and vscode/package.json
  - %vscodeManifest [./vscode/package.json][1] // keep the extension version aligned
- package-lock.json and vscode/package-lock.json are in sync
- bump the version before publishing the npm package or the .vsix

# npm package for cli [./package.json][1]
- publish the CLI to the npm registry
- provide npm.sh at the repository root
  - %npm.sh [./npm.sh][1] // executable bash entry point
  - run from any working directory and resolve its own location
  - use bash with set -euo pipefail
  - require node and npm; fail with a clear message if either is missing
  - use a project-local .npm-cache so a broken global npm cache does not block publishing
  - install dependencies with npm ci
  - run npm test and npm run typecheck
  - build the CLI bundle with npm run build:cli
  - verify the package with npm pack --dry-run
  - verify publish readiness with npm publish --dry-run
  - check npm whoami; fail with a message to run npm login when not authenticated
  - ask for explicit confirmation before the real npm publish
  - never print or store the npm token; use the human's npm login or NODE_AUTH_TOKEN
  - exit nonzero if any step fails
- package setup for the CLI
  - bundle src/cli.ts with esbuild into dist/cli.js, platform node, format esm, target node20
  - point bin.onlyhuman at dist/cli.js and keep the node shebang
  - add root esbuild as a devDependency and build:cli to scripts
  - the CLI runtime has no npm dependencies; keep the vscode-languageserver packages in devDependencies for the LSP source
  - set files to dist, README.md and LICENSE; no src, tests, vscode or node_modules in the package
  - remove private: true; npm refuses to publish a private package
  - set engines.node to >=20 for the bundle; keep development at >=22.18
  - keep the MIT license and add description, repository, homepage, bugs, keywords and author
  - add prepublishOnly = npm test && npm run typecheck && npm run build:cli
  - check `npm view onlyhuman version`; if the name is taken, use the scoped name "@benjit87/onlyhuman" with publishConfig.access public
  - when using the fallback scope, publish with npm publish --access public
- alternative: keep bin.onlyhuman at src/cli.ts and ship src/, requiring Node >=22.18; no esbuild step
- decisions for the human
  - package name and scope
  - whether to publish the parser API as imports, not just the CLI
  - the minimum Node version for CLI users

$Data [./src/types.ts][1]
- *.human file, utf-8, lf or crlf
- *.bot file, same syntax as *.human, so the same tokenizer and parser read both
- processed line by line, blank lines are skipped
- a line is one of
  - header // no leading -, starts with a prefix
  - child // starts with - after optional indent
  - blank
- in a *.bot a header may end with a pointer, e.g `$Token [./src/types.ts][15]`
  - the pointer is two words glued together, [path][line], so it needs no new token

$Structure [./src/parser.ts][6]
- each child line starts with exactly one bullet marker
- each deeper child adds 2 spaces before its bullet marker
- nesting is represented by indentation, never by repeated bullet markers on one line
- depth = indent level + 1 if the line has a bullet, header depth = 0
- indent uses spaces only, exactly 2 per level; any other indentation is an error
  - a tab or a space count that is not a multiple of 2 is an error, see bad-indent
- parent = nearest previous line with smaller depth
- unlimited depth
- only the first - on a line is the bullet, any later - is a - token
- a line with only one operator after its child marker lists that operator; an optional // comment may follow
- a listed dash is the child marker followed by a dash token and an optional comment
- *.human and *.bot use the same child and indentation rules
- consecutive-header is an error: a prefix header must have at least one child line before the next prefix header; blank lines do not count

$Token [./src/types.ts][16]
- every token has kind, text, line, col, length // 1-based line, 0-based col
- prefixes are $ @ ? and #
  - prefix token, followed by word as its name when attached e.g $Token = $ + word(Token)
  - alone is valid e.g `- $` inside $Token
  - parentheses adjacent to a prefix are punctuation, not part of the name
    - `(@Parser)` tokenizes as `(`, `@Parser`, `)`, so the symbol stays visible to go-to-definition and linting
    - a run that starts with a quote stays one word, so `'(?Parsed)'` remains a quoted example rather than a symbol reference
- comment marker // is a double slash
  - starts a comment, rest of line is one comment_sentence token
- operators are arrow // ->
- operators are plus // +
- operators are equals // ==
  - space separated
- dash // -
  - bullet if first on the line, else a - token
- percent // %, marks a method or additional detail on a child line, never a header
  - attached to the name, e.g %tokenize, no space
  - the tokenizer emits it in any file, only the *.bot grammar highlights it
- "import"
  - "import module.human as @<alias>" // the @ is required, a bare alias is plain text
    - as @<alias> gives the imported spec a local name; its definitions are then referenced as "@alias.api_service"
    - the alias is the word after as, an @ followed by one word of letters, digits and underscore, no `.` in it
    - an import with no as @<alias> stays the plain form, every definition counts as defined here unqualified
  - "@alias.api_service" // refers to `@api_service` of module.human, the alias is a qualifier that calls its service
    - tokenized as the prefix @, the word alias and the glued word .api_service, so go-to-definition and autocomplete can tell the two parts apart
  - "@alias.$Name" // a `$` or `?` name of module.human, the prefix after the dot picks the kind, no prefix means @
    - tokenized as the prefix @, the word alias and the glued word starting with a dot, then an optional $ or ? prefix, then the name
    - "@alias.$Structure" refers to `$Structure` of module.human, "@alias.?Parsed" to `?Parsed`, "@alias.Api" to `@Api`
    - # is not accepted after the dot, a requirement statement is not a name
  - @<alias> // an @ definition made by the import line, see @Linter
  - import is a keyword token, only when it is the first word of a line, either bare or after a bullet, e.g `import backend.human` or `- import backend.human`
  - the next word is the file name, one file per line, resolved under the spec/ folder; a missing .human extension is not added
  - anywhere else on a line, or inside a comment_sentence, import is an ordinary word
  - a bare import line is exempt from no-prefix and consecutive-header, so it can start a line with no prefix
  - meaning: every definition ($ @ ? #) of the named spec counts as defined in this file, see @Linter and @GoToDefinition
- word // can represent methods or a defined behavior or just a sentence, semantically driven
  - any other run of non-space characters e.g *.human, tokenize
  - a // is a comment only when it starts a space-separated run
    - `"//"`, `a//b` and `\//` are words, not comments, so the line goes on
    - quotes are not special, `"// x"` is two words `"//` and `x"`, any character before // does it
    - the highlighter follows the same rule, a // after a non-space character is not a comment
    - to write a literal name or operator in a *.bot without lint treating it as a symbol, quote it, e.g `"$Name"` or `"->"`
  - meaning is read by the robot, code only tokenizes it
- comment_sentence // the words after //, this represent instructions or remarks
  - kept as one raw string, never tokenized further, never a reference
  - instructions for the robot when generating, ignored by lint and go-to-definition

$Tokens [./src/types.ts][25]
- $Token // list, in source order, line breaks kept via line number

$Node [./src/types.ts][29]
- $Token // the token of this line, first token after bullet
- tokens // all $Token of the line, in order
- depth
- children // $Node list, defined by - bullets and indent
- kind // header | child

?Data [./src/tokenizer.ts][8]
- $Data

?Parsed [./src/types.ts][40]
- AST of $Node // tree of nodes, one per line
- roots = header nodes, children nest by $Structure
- a child with no header above it is kept at root and flagged by @Linter
- parse never throws, bad input still yields a tree

@Parser [./src/parser.ts][16]
- $Data -> tokenize -> $Tokens
- $Tokens + $Structure -> parser -> ?Parsed
- tokenize(text) returns $Tokens
- parse(tokens) returns ?Parsed
- method signatures
  - %tokenize(text: string): Tokens [./src/tokenizer.ts][8]
    - input is the whole file text, lf or crlf, blank lines are skipped
    - emits every $Token of a line in source order with line 1-based, col 0-based, length and the line indent
    - a // starts a comment_sentence only when it begins a space-separated run, an import is marked only as the first word of a line with exactly one file name
    - never throws, unknown text becomes word tokens
  - %parse(tokens: Tokens): Parsed [./src/parser.ts][16]
    - groups tokens into one $Node per line and links parent and children by indent depth, 2 spaces per level
    - a child with no header above it stays at root, bad indentation is kept in the tree for @Linter to report
    - never throws, bad input still yields a tree
  - %parseText(text: string): Parsed [./src/parser.ts][53] // tokenize + parse in one call
    - the entry point used by @Linter, @GoToDefinition, @LSP and @CLI

@Linter [./src/linter.ts][21]
- ?Parsed -> lint -> diagnostics
- method signatures
  - %lint(parsed: Parsed, options: LintOptions = {}): Diagnostic[] [./src/linter.ts][21] // pure: parse tree + optional file info -> diagnostics, sorted by line then col
    - runs every rule in the rules list below on one parse tree, the same for *.human and *.bot
    - reads definitions from the tree plus the imported specs given by resolveImport, never reads a file itself
    - returns an empty list when there is nothing to report, never throws
  - %LintOptions [./src/linter.ts][6] // optional file info enables missing-robot, missing-human, broken-pointer and unresolved-import; resolveImport(file) returns the parsed imported spec or none, so the linter stays free of fs
    - file = path, siblingExists, lineCount(pointerPath) and resolveImport(name)
    - without file, the four file rules are skipped
- diagnostic = rule, severity, line, col, length, message
- definition = a header whose first token is $ ? or @ plus a name
- reference = $ ? or @ plus a name anywhere outside a comment, names are case-sensitive and per prefix, so $Data != ?Data
- the same rules run on *.human and *.bot
- import <file>.human imports all relevant definitions
  - import <file>.human as @<alias> imports them under the alias, references must be "@alias.name", see "qualified imports" below
  - every $ @ ? # definition of the imported spec counts as defined here, so undefined-reference does not fire on it
  - imported definitions are never reported as unused-definition, and a local definition with the same name wins, not a duplicate-definition
  - imports are one level only, the imported spec's own imports are not followed, so there are no cycles
  - qualified imports
    - a reference "@alias.api_service", with its prefix, counts as defined when the imported spec defines `@api_service`
    - a reference "@alias.$Name" or "@alias.?Name" counts as defined when the imported spec defines `$Name` or `?Name`, the prefix after the dot picks the kind and none means @
    - the key looked up in the imported spec is that prefix + name, so `$Name` never matches `@Name`
    - the alias is an @ definition made by the import line, so a bare `@alias` anywhere also counts as a reference to it
    - an alias that no "@alias.name" reference uses is an unused-definition warning, reported on the alias in the import line
    - a qualified reference to a name the imported spec does not define is an undefined-reference
    - a reference to an alias that no import declares is an undefined-reference
    - the definitions of an aliased import are not defined unqualified, `@api_service` alone is an undefined-reference unless defined locally or by a plain import
    - two imports with the same alias, or an alias equal to a local @ header name, is a duplicate-definition on the second
    - `import a.human as be` without the @ is plain text, not an import, so it reports nothing as an import
- file info, when the caller has it = path, whether the sibling exists, and a way to count lines of a pointed file, so the linter itself stays free of fs
- rules
  - no-prefix // error: header line with no - and no prefix e.g `Parser`
  - orphan-child // error: - line before any header
  - bad-indent // error: tab, or leading spaces not a multiple of 2
  - indent-jump // error: child deeper than parent depth + 1
  - repeated-bullet // error: a dash token followed by other content after the child marker; use indentation for nesting
  - consecutive-header // error: a prefix header followed by another prefix header with no child line between them; blank lines do not count
  - undefined-reference // error: reference with no definition in this file or in an imported spec e.g $Nodee
  - duplicate-definition // error: same prefix + name defined twice, reported on the second
  - dangling-operator // error: an operator missing a left or right operand
  - unused-definition // warning: defined but never referenced
  - missing-robot // warning: x.human has no sibling x.bot, only when the caller passes file info
  - missing-human // warning: x.bot has no sibling x.human, only when the caller passes file info
  - unresolved-import // error: an import line whose file is missing, or has no file name, only when the caller passes file info
  - broken-pointer // warning: a *.bot header whose [path][line] points to a missing file, or a line past the end of it
    - [path] is relative to the nearest folder that holds .git, found by walking up from the spec file
    - when no .git is found, [path] is relative to the parent of the spec/ folder
    - only checked when the caller passes file info
- not flagged
  - a bare $ @ ? with no name e.g `- $` in $Token
  - bare words e.g tokenize, they are behaviour names
  - # lines, they are requirement statements, not definitions
  - anything inside a comment_sentence
  - a line that is only one operator, e.g under $Token, it is a listing
  - quoted text like `"$Name"`, it is a word

@GoToDefinition [./src/definition.ts][139]
- (?Parsed, position) -> find token -> definition location
- (?Parsed, name) -> definition location
- name = prefix + name, e.g $Token
- method signatures
  - %collectSymbols(parsed: Parsed): SymbolRef[] [./src/definition.ts][14] // shared definition and reference scan, also used by @Linter
    - returns one SymbolRef per attached $ @ ? prefix and name, with key, location and isDefinition
    - isDefinition is true only when the symbol is the first token of a header
  - %findDefinition(parsed: Parsed, key: string): Location | undefined [./src/definition.ts][77] // by name, key = prefix + name
    - a key starting with # matches the full requirement text, otherwise the first definition with that key wins
    - returns none when there is no such definition
  - %definitionAt(parsed: Parsed, line: number, col: number, robot?: Parsed): Location | undefined [./src/definition.ts][139] // by position, in a *.human also a % child line to the same % child line in the robot
    - order of checks: import file name, % child line, # header, then $ @ ? symbol
    - a definition header or % child goes to the robot when it is passed and has the match, a reference goes to its .human header, an unresolved reference tries the imported specs
    - the cursor in a comment returns none, the function does no file reading
  - %importAt(parsed: Parsed, line: number, col: number): string | undefined [./src/definition.ts][71] // on the file name of an import line, returns that file name so the caller opens it at line 1; the cursor on the alias after as does the same
    - returns none when the cursor is not on an import file name
  - %pointerAt(parsed: Parsed, line: number, col: number): Pointer | undefined [./src/definition.ts][226] // in a *.bot, header or % line to its [path][line]
    - the cursor must be on the % or its name for a % line, or on the definition name for a header
    - returns none when the line has no pointer, the caller checks that the file exists
  - %parsePointer(token: Token): Pointer | undefined [./src/definition.ts][111] // parse the glued [path][line] word without reading the file
  - %pointerOf(node: Node): Pointer | undefined [./src/definition.ts][117] // find the first pointer in a header or % child line
    - returns the first token of the line that parses as [path][line], none if there is none
  - %methodAt(parsed: Parsed, line: number, col: number) [./src/definition.ts][189] // private helper of definitionAt for the % child rule
    - the cursor must be on the % or on the name glued to it, returns the node, the name and the location
  - %headerKey(node: Node): string | undefined [./src/definition.ts][199] // private helper, prefix + name or # + requirement text of a header
  - %robotMethod(parsed: Parsed, node: Node, robot: Parsed, name: string): Location | undefined [./src/definition.ts][208] // private helper, finds the same %name under the same top-level header in the robot, any pointer is ignored
- found: returns line, col, length of the defining header
- not found, or token is not an $ ? @ reference or an eligible header: returns none
- parentheses around a prefixed symbol do not block navigation, e.g `(@Parser)` and `(?Parsed)` resolve on the symbol
- duplicates: first definition wins, @Linter reports the rest
- cursor in a comment returns none
- scope is one *.human file plus its sibling *.bot, plus the files named by its import lines, no other files
- in a *.human
  - a reference goes to its .human header
  - a reference to an imported name goes to its header in the imported file
  - a qualified reference "@alias.name", "@alias.$Name" or "@alias.?Name" goes to the matching header in the file imported as alias, when the cursor is on the dot part
  - the cursor on the `@alias` part of "@alias.name", or on a bare @<alias> reference, goes to the import line that defines the alias
  - cursor on the alias after as, or on the file name, goes to the imported file at line 1
  - import file.human, with the cursor on the file name, goes to the target file at line 1
  - if the target file is missing, stays on the import line
  - cursor on a definition header of $ @ ? or # goes to the matching header in x.bot
  - # headers match by their full requirement text after #, excluding a trailing [path][line] pointer in x.bot
  - the robot header is found by parsing x.bot with the same @Parser, trailing [path][line] on the header is ignored
  - found: location carries the robot file, so @LSP and @CLI can open another file
  - no x.bot, or no such header in it: stays on the .human definition
  - cursor on a % child line goes to the same % child line in x.bot, under the same parent header
    - the parent header is found as for a definition header, then its children are searched for the same %name
    - the same %name under another header is not a match
    - %name matches by name only, any trailing [path][line] pointer in x.bot is ignored
    - no x.bot, no such parent header, or no such % child under it: stays on the .human line
  - // comment headers have no robot target
- in a *.bot
  - a reference goes to its .bot header
  - cursor on a definition header goes to the code line in its [path][line]
  - cursor on a % child line goes to the code file and line in its trailing [path][line], same rule as a header
  - no pointer, or the file is missing: stays on the .bot header
- the caller passes the parsed robot, the function does no file reading

@AutoComplete [./src/autocomplete.ts][39]
- the @<alias> in import statement must have autocomplete function
- triggers on @ and on "@alias."
- offer the @<alias> of this spec's imports, then the @ $ ? names of the imported spec
- works in *.human and *.bot
- method signatures
  - %completeAt(parsed: Parsed, line: number, col: number, resolveImport?: (name: string) => Parsed | undefined): CompletionItem[] [./src/autocomplete.ts][39] // in src/autocomplete.ts, pure like @GoToDefinition, no fs
    - after `@` alone, items are the aliases declared by the import lines, then the @ definitions of this spec
    - after `$` or `?` alone, items are the definitions of that prefix in this spec
    - after "@alias." items are the @ definitions of the spec imported as alias, name only, the prefix and alias are already typed
    - after "@alias.$" items are the $ definitions and after "@alias.?" the ? definitions of that spec, name only
    - no items in a comment_sentence, and none after "@alias." when the alias is unknown or its file is missing
    - an item carries the label, the kind (alias, service, entity, state) and the file it comes from
  - %onCompletion(params) [./src/lsp/server.ts][104] // in src/lsp/server.ts, calls completeAt with the import resolver for the file on disk
    - %onInitialize announces completionProvider with the trigger characters @ $ ? and .

@LSP [./src/lsp/server.ts][29]
- @GoToDefinition
- @Linter
- @AutoComplete
- transport ipc or stdio, library vscode-languageserver, full text sync
- on open and on change: parse, lint, textDocument/publishDiagnostics
- textDocument/definition -> @GoToDefinition
- textDocument/completion -> @AutoComplete
- positions 0-based, per the lsp protocol
- method signatures
  - %onInitialize [./src/lsp/server.ts][29] // announces full text sync, definitionProvider and completionProvider
  - %validate(doc: TextDocument): void [./src/lsp/server.ts][37] // parse the text, lint it with file info when the uri is a file:, publish the diagnostics
    - lint line 1-based to lsp line 0-based, severity error or warning, code is the rule, source is onlyhuman
  - %onDidOpen and %onDidChangeContent [./src/lsp/server.ts][63] // call validate on every open and change
  - %onDefinition(params) [./src/lsp/server.ts][66] // for a *.bot try @GoToDefinition pointerAt first and open the code file at the pointer line when it exists
    - otherwise call definitionAt, passing the sibling robot for a *.human and the import resolver, and answer with the robot, imported or current file
    - answers null when there is no document or no location
- vscode client in ./vscode starts the server for files *.human, and *.bot

@Skill [./src/skill.ts][1]
- source is the onlyhuman/SKILL.md that ships inside the onlyhuman library, fixed in the library, not looked up from the user path
  - %initSkill(directory: string = ".", options?: InitSkillOptions): string [./src/skill.ts][13] // copy the library skill file into <directory>/onlyhuman/SKILL.md, return the target path
    - throws when the library file is missing, or when the target exists and options.force is not set
    - creates the onlyhuman directory when missing, then copies byte-for-byte
  - %InitSkillOptions [./src/skill.ts][6] // force?: boolean, allow replacing an existing target
  - the source path is resolved from the library's own location, e.g. import.meta.url, never from <directory> or the current directory
  - onlyhuman/SKILL.md is listed in package.json files so it is present after npm i
  - fail with a clear error when the library skill file cannot be found
- copy as is, no generating or editing
  - byte-for-byte with copyFileSync
- nothing from the user repo is used
  - a SKILL.md in the user repo is neither read nor replaced by the source
- target is onlyhuman/SKILL.md under the user path
  - create the onlyhuman directory when missing
  - refuse to overwrite an existing target without --force
  - print the written path
- validation
  - the source onlyhuman/SKILL.md must pass skill-creator quick_validate.py
  - onlyhuman lint validates .human and .bot specs, not Markdown
- tests assert the copied file matches onlyhuman/SKILL.md and ignores a different SKILL.md in the user project

@CLI [./src/cli.ts][29]
- @GoToDefinition
- @Linter
- @Skill
- method signatures
  - %main(argv: string[]): number [./src/cli.ts][29] // dispatch lint, def and skill init, returns the exit code, prints the usage and returns 2 on a bad command
  - %load(file: string): Parsed [./src/cli.ts][25] // read the file as utf-8 and parseText it
  - %notFound(what: string): number [./src/cli.ts][99] // print "no definition for <what>" to stderr and return 1
- onlyhuman skill init
  - %skillInit [./src/cli.ts][32] // parse the command and call initSkill
    - at most one directory argument, more than one prints the usage and returns 2
    - an initSkill error is printed to stderr and returns 1, success prints "created <path>" and returns 0
  - copy the library skill file as is to onlyhuman/SKILL.md under the given directory
  - create onlyhuman when missing
  - fail if the library skill file is missing
  - do not overwrite an existing file without --force
  - print the written path
- onlyhuman lint <file>
  - one line per diagnostic: file:line:col severity rule message
  - exit 1 if any error, else 0, warnings do not fail
  - accepts *.human and *.bot
- onlyhuman def <name> <file>
  - prints file:line:col of the definition
- onlyhuman def <file>:<line>:<col>
  - same, from a cursor position
  - exit 1 if not found
- positions 1-based in the cli output
- def supports both name form and position form
- the cli passes file info, so missing-robot, missing-human, broken-pointer and unresolved-import are checked on lint, and imported specs are read from disk
- @LSP passes file info too when the file is on disk

// assumptions, open points in spec.human, change spec.human if wrong
// 1. @GoToDefinition and @Linter list their public method or grammar-rule signatures with brief explanations; private helpers are omitted
// 2. superseded, @Highlighter is no longer defined in spec.human, see 66
// 3. superseded, see 66; the TextMate scope notes now live in vscode.bot
// 4. the vscode deployment section moved to vscode.human; its generated spec and assumptions are in vscode.bot
// 5. development uses Node >= 22.18; the bundled server targets the Node runtime shipped with VS Code, and the CLI bundle targets node20
// 6. in a *.bot, go-to-definition on a header or % line goes to its code pointer, not back to the .human header; the .human definition remains the source of intent
// 7. npm.sh uses a project-local .npm-cache so the publish flow does not depend on the state of the global npm cache
// 8. the CLI bundle uses esbuild and targets Node 20 even though development uses Node 22.18; the source-TypeScript alternative keeps Node >=22.18
// 9. package name availability is not checked until publish; the current name is onlyhuman and the scoped fallback is "@benjit87/onlyhuman"
// 10. the npm package exports only the CLI; the parser API remains internal until the human asks for it
// 11. # version control applies 0.8.0 to package.json, vscode/package.json and both lockfiles
// 12. @Skill is exposed as onlyhuman skill init and writes onlyhuman/SKILL.md under the given directory
// 13. skill init refuses to overwrite an existing onlyhuman/SKILL.md unless --force is passed
// 14. @Skill in spec.human has no flow line now; --force, create-directory and print-path behaviour are kept from the previous spec as details
// 15. "fixed in the library" is read as a file shipped in the npm package and located relative to the running code, not a copy embedded in source
// 16. the target is literally onlyhuman/SKILL.md; existing .onlyhuman/SKILL.md files are left in place and are not migrated
// 17. consecutive-header applies only to prefix headers (@ ? $ #); comment headers are not counted, and a final header with no child is not flagged unless a separate empty-header rule is added
// 18. the example '(?Parser)' is read as a state name; ?Parser is undefined in this spec, so the example covers recognition inside parentheses, not a successful lookup
// 19. only parentheses are treated as ignored punctuation; other punctuation such as commas remains a separate word
// 20. "import" is now tool-supported: @Linter, @GoToDefinition and the grammar in vscode.bot handle it; before this regen it was a robot-only convention
// 21. "*.human" in the import line is read as a placeholder for a spec file name such as backend.human, resolved under spec/, not a glob; "import all" means every $ @ ? # header of that file
// 22. "starts in a new line" is read as: import is the first word of its own line, one file per line; it may be bare (no prefix, exempt from no-prefix) or a child line after a bullet
// 23. "relevant definitions" is read as all definitions of the imported spec; unused-definition is not applied to imported names
// 24. the human did not say what happens when the file is missing, so unresolved-import is added as an error, only when file info is passed
// 25. imports are one level only, so no cycle rule is needed; transitive imports and cycle detection are left out
// 26. a local definition with the same name as an imported one wins and is not reported as a duplicate
// 27. go-to-definition on a reference to an imported name jumps to the imported file; the human only wrote "import file.human, goes to the target file", so this is an extension
// 28. the method pointers for %importAt and the unresolved-import rule were checked against the code on this regen and resolve to existing lines
// 29. spec.human lines 45 and 97 spell "definations"; read as "definitions", fix the spelling in spec.human if unintended
// 30. a % child line in a *.human is matched to a % child of the same name under the same parent header in the *.bot, not globally, since one name can appear under several headers
// 31. when the *.bot has no matching % child, go-to-definition stays on the .human line, same as for headers
// 32. % stays a child-line token only, never a header, so the linter and PREFIXES are unchanged
// 33. the % child rule is implemented in definitionAt through methodAt and robotMethod, with a test in tests/core.test.ts; pointers were updated after coding
// 34. "a % in a *.human has no robot target" from the previous bot is removed, it is superseded by the new % child line rule
// 35. version 0.7.0 was applied on an earlier approve, 0.8.0 was applied on this one to package.json, vscode/package.json and both lockfiles, only the package's own version lines, not dependency versions
// 36. "describe every method under each service name in details via %" is read as: every exported method of a service gets a % child line with a short detail list; private helpers are listed only for @GoToDefinition because the new % rule lives in them
// 37. superseded, see 66
// 38. the fs helpers in src/files.ts are shared by @LSP and @CLI but belong to no service in spec.human, so they are not listed as service methods
// 39. spec.human no longer has the "import core.human" line; the earlier unresolved import is gone, so spec.bot carries no import
// 40. the #vscode deployment section is no longer in spec.human, so it was removed from this file; it now lives in vscode.human and vscode.bot
// 41. the layout list under #implementation still names the vscode/ files, since spec.human still says the implementation lives in those paths
// 42. no code changed in this regen, so the [path][line] pointers were kept; lint checks that they still resolve
// 43. the human wrote the alias as `as @<alias>` and the @ is required; `as be` without the @ is plain text, not an import
// 44. "an alias is a qualifier that calls its service" is read as: a name of the imported spec is called as "@alias.name", e.g "@alias.api_service" calls `@api_service`
// 45. the human did not say whether an aliased import also imports names unqualified; read as no, they are only reachable as "@alias.name"
// 46. @<alias> is an @ definition made by the import line, not a header, so it is checked for unused-definition and duplicate-definition, and a bare @alias counts as a reference to it
// 47. the plain import <file>.human stays as in the previous regen; spec.human line "import <file>.human imports every definition unqualified" confirms it
// 48. an unknown alias, a missing name under an alias, and a duplicate alias use the existing undefined-reference and duplicate-definition rules, no new rule was added
// 49. imports stay one level only, an aliased import does not make the imported spec's own imports available
// 50. an alias is @ plus one word of letters, digits and underscore; `import a.human as @` with no word, a dotted alias or extra words is plain text
// 51. @<alias> gets its own TextMate scope variable.other.alias.human after as and in "@alias.name"; the human said "different color", the editor theme still maps the scope to a color
// 52. the cursor on the .name part of "@alias.name" goes to the name in the imported file, on the @alias part it goes to the import line; spec.human lists both lines without saying which part of the text each is for
// 53. @AutoComplete lives in src/autocomplete.ts (completeAt) and is wired in src/lsp/server.ts (onCompletion, completionProvider); the pointers were set after coding
// 54. autocomplete offers names only, no snippets or documentation, and the trigger characters are @ $ ? and .
// 55. "do not call onlyhuman cli" is read as: no onlyhuman lint or def command is run while building, whether from src/cli.ts, dist/cli.js or npx; the linter and definition core are exercised through npm test only
// 56. because of 55 this spec.bot was not linted with the onlyhuman cli, which the skill asks for before presenting; check it by hand or tell me to lint it
// 57. as @<alias> is implemented as of this approve: tokenizer, definition, linter, grammar and autocomplete; a bare `as be` is plain text now; the vscode client bundle and the .vsix were not rebuilt
// 58. a bare @alias that nothing defines is an undefined-reference like any other name; the alias itself is an @ definition made by the import line
// 59. autocomplete only looks at the tokens before the cursor on its line, so the cursor in the middle of a typed name offers nothing; after @ it offers aliases then this spec's @ definitions, after @alias. only @ definitions of the imported spec
// 60. "the prefix after the dot picks the kind, no prefix means @" is read as: "@alias.$Name" is `$Name`, "@alias.?Name" is `?Name`, "@alias.Name" is `@Name`; a # or an @ after the dot is plain text
// 61. a $ or ? name reached through an alias gets the same checks as an @ name: an unknown name or alias is an undefined-reference, and the reference counts as a use of the alias
// 62. "@alias.$Name" and "@alias.?Name" are implemented as of this approve in the symbol scan, linter, go-to-definition, autocomplete and grammar; the vscode client bundle and the .vsix were not rebuilt
// 63. spec.bot was not linted with the onlyhuman cli, see 55; the "@alias.$Name" examples above are quoted so they are not read as references
// 64. spec.human says version 0.8.0; it was applied to package.json, vscode/package.json and both package-lock.json files on approve, only the package's own version lines
// 65. the .vsix for 0.8.0 is named onlyhuman-vscode-0.8.0.vsix and is built only when asked, it is not part of approve
// 66. @Highlighter moved from spec.human to vscode.human and the line naming it was removed from #implementation, so spec.human no longer has an undefined reference; "work on *.bot as well as *.human" now covers @Linter and @GoToDefinition only
// 67. the per-token colour list that was under @Highlighter in spec.human was dropped by the human; the grammar itself is unchanged and keeps its scopes, see vscode.bot
// 68. unused-definition warnings for ?Data, @Parser, @LSP and @CLI are left as they are, spec.human is not changed by the robot
// 69. onlyhuman-skill/SKILL.md was moved to onlyhuman/SKILL.md by the human and the two @Skill lines in spec.human say so; applied on approve to src/skill.ts, src/cli.ts help, package.json files, tests and README
// 70. no code changed in this regen, so the [path][line] pointers were kept; the pointers of the removed @Highlighter section moved to vscode.bot
// 71. spec.human line 3 still says "only read in onlyhuman-skill/SKILL.md"; that path no longer exists, so it is read as onlyhuman/SKILL.md; fix line 3 if the old path was meant
// 72. the source inside the package and the target in the user project both use the folder onlyhuman/, as the two @Skill lines say; the folder name matches the package name, so an existing onlyhuman/ folder in a user project is treated as the skill folder, and an existing SKILL.md there is not overwritten without --force
// 73. the path rename is not migrated for users; an existing onlyhuman-skill/SKILL.md in a user project is left in place
// 74. applied on approve: the source file is onlyhuman/SKILL.md in the repo; the old onlyhuman-skill/ entry shows as deleted in git and onlyhuman/ is untracked, nothing was staged or committed; dist/cli.js was rebuilt, the vscode bundle and .vsix were not; the [path][line] pointers were rechecked and the edits did not change any line numbers
